Complete Case Study: Agentic AI Intelligent Loan Approval System
1. Problem Statement
In modern banking systems, loan approval requires evaluating applicant details, credit history, risk indicators, and regulatory rules across multiple systems. Manual or rule-heavy approaches can be slow, inconsistent, and difficult to scale.
This case study focuses on designing and implementing a multi-agent Agentic AI system that analyzes loan applications and classifies them as:
Approved
Rejected
Requires Manual Review
2. Business Objectives
Automate loan application analysis using Agentic AI.
Improve decision speed and consistency.
Provide explainable and auditable decisions.
Adopt a scalable, loosely coupled microservices architecture.
3. Input Parameters — Loan Application Data
The system receives the following application fields:
Applicant ID
Applicant Profile: age, income, and employment type
Credit Score
Loan Amount and Tenure
Existing Liabilities
Location
Application Timestamp
4. Proposed Solution — Multi-Agent Architecture
The solution follows a distributed, microservices-based Agentic AI architecture. Independent agents collaborate through an orchestration layer and access data and tools through standardized interfaces.
4.1 Presentation Layer
Technology: Streamlit
Provides a chatbot-style user interface for loan application submission and status display.
4.2 Microservice Layer
Technology: FastAPI
Exposes REST endpoints to receive and validate application data.
4.3 Orchestration Layer
Technology: LangGraph
Coordinates agent workflows.
Manages shared workflow state.
Controls decision routing and transitions between agents.
4.4 Agent Layer
Contains domain-specific agents, each responsible for a defined part of the loan evaluation lifecycle.
4.5 Communication Layer
Technology: MCP (Model Context Protocol)
Provides standardized access to tools and services through MCP servers.
5. Multi-Agent System Workflow
The user submits loan application data through the Streamlit chatbot UI.
The UI forwards the data to a FastAPI-based microservice.
The microservice validates the request and passes it to the Agentic AI orchestration engine.
The orchestrator invokes relevant agents and tools through MCP.
Agents retrieve permitted contextual data, perform analysis, and return structured results.
The orchestrator synthesizes the results and invokes the LLM where appropriate.
The final result is returned to the chatbot UI for display.
6. Agent Responsibilities
6.1 Applicant Profile Agent
MCP Server: ApplicantDB
Responsibility: Assess the supplied applicant profile and retrieve relevant applicant records through authorized tools.
Outputs:
Income Stability Score
Employment Risk
Credit History Summary
Application Completeness Flags
6.2 Financial Risk Analysis Agent
MCP Server: RiskRulesDB
Responsibility: Evaluate financial affordability and risk indicators using validated calculations and applicable risk rules.
Outputs:
Debt-to-Income Ratio
Credit Score Risk Level
Loan Amount Risk
Anomaly Detection
Reasoning
6.3 Loan Decision Agent
MCP Server: DecisionSynthesis
Responsibility: Combine the analysis results and applicable lending rules to produce a decision classification and supporting explanation.
Outputs:
Classification: Approve / Reject / Review
Risk Score
Confidence Level
Key Decision Factors
Explanation
6.4 Compliance & Action Orchestrator Agent
MCP Server: NotificationSystem
Responsibility: Coordinate permitted post-decision actions, notifications, and audit records.
Outputs:
Action Taken
Notification Sent
Case ID
Timestamp
Summary
