8. Evaluation Criteria
Each participant must submit their own implementation and demonstrate:
Understanding of Agentic AI architecture.
Correct orchestration using LangGraph.
Clear agent responsibilities and MCP usage.
Ability to modify code live during evaluation.
Explainable AI outputs.
9. Evaluation Process
Internal technical evaluation through a one-on-one session.
Live code walkthrough during the discussion.
Relative performance scoring.
10. Expected System Output
The system is expected to return one of the following classifications:
Approved
Rejected
Requires Manual Review
The result should include the relevant risk assessment, confidence information, key decision factors, and an explanation, as specified in the agent responsibilities.
11. Important Implementation Considerations
The case study defines the architecture and expected outputs, but does not specify actual lending thresholds, credit-score cutoffs, risk-score weights, or a complete regulatory policy. These must be defined and validated before real-world use.
A reliable implementation should:
Use deterministic and validated calculations for financial metrics.
Apply documented lending rules consistently.
Ground AI-generated explanations in verified input data and agent findings.
Route incomplete, conflicting, or exceptional cases to manual review.
Maintain appropriate access controls and auditable decision records.